\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The two families across the spin range (solid: drainhole, $\eta _0=2$, $\gamma =-\pi $; dashed: symmetric, $\eta _0=1$, $\gamma =0$), against $v_e$: (a) mass, angular momentum and equatorial throat radius; (b) $J/M^2$ against the Kerr bound; (c) the ratio of end masses; (d) the flattening $R_p/R_e$; (e) the scalar charge; (f) the throat angular velocity, which peaks and falls to zero at extremality. Grey verticals in (a): the spins of the first two drainhole holds. The symmetric family violates the Kerr bound at every spin and the drainhole family exceeds it by up to $\sim 20\%$ at intermediate spin; both families end on extremal Kerr.}}{5}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:trends}{{3}{5}{The two families across the spin range (solid: drainhole, $\eta _0=2$, $\gamma =-\pi $; dashed: symmetric, $\eta _0=1$, $\gamma =0$), against $v_e$: (a) mass, angular momentum and equatorial throat radius; (b) $J/M^2$ against the Kerr bound; (c) the ratio of end masses; (d) the flattening $R_p/R_e$; (e) the scalar charge; (f) the throat angular velocity, which peaks and falls to zero at extremality. Grey verticals in (a): the spins of the first two drainhole holds. The symmetric family violates the Kerr bound at every spin and the drainhole family exceeds it by up to $\sim 20\%$ at intermediate spin; both families end on extremal Kerr}{figure.3}{}}
